#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064994} :activity/id #uuid "b309af6c-4786-5404-a3b4-0a5fc0d4a4ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048162 :topic/id #uuid "840e1871-5648-5ed4-855a-86ff3a9aa7a4" :topic/math-academy-id 364} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065348 :tutorial/content "We know how to use the first derivative test to find the local extrema of a continuous function. But how do we find the global extrema of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]?$\n\nWe apply the **candidates test**, which consists of the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1**:  Find all of the critical points. The critical points are our \"candidates.\"\n\n- If the function is defined on a closed interval $[a,b],$ then we must remember to include the endpoints as well.\n\n- If the function is defined on an open interval $(a,b),$ then we do not include the endpoints.\n\n**Step 2**: Determine the value of the function at each of the critical points.\n\n**Step 3**: Compare all the values of $f$ found in step $2.$ The greatest of these values is the global maximum value, and the least is the global minimum value." :tutorial/id #uuid "e714b234-f163-5ae5-a064-6bef2d3b9fc1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9b5f6fdc-d0a8-5c91-a879-4f798a936a36" :step/math-academy-id 12797 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064995}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064045 :question/id #uuid "69180c3b-7c26-5e5f-8d7a-42aed98431bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3736"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446080}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval"} :step/id #uuid "8149edd7-e4fe-579d-804f-4e9bf9911f55" :step/math-academy-id 12798 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064164 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064046 :question/id #uuid "a0f47cd2-ea8f-5278-ab67-46ecd65d2f4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3737"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292686} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446079}] :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"} :step/id #uuid "5743e9b2-be38-579c-8843-93e55b3e92f2" :step/math-academy-id 12799}] :activity/title "The Candidates Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064451}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101725} :activity/id #uuid "6bc26614-e494-5e0e-b8e8-0405e49dbaa9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578 :topic/id #uuid "aae56e33-e57d-5843-b31c-fcba98a1ca50" :topic/math-academy-id 1163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102033 :tutorial/content "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $y=y(t)$ is called a **logistic growth equation.** \n\nNote the following:\n\n- $a$ is a positive constant called the **carrying capacity**.\n\n- $k$ is a positive constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below:\n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nWe will learn more about the structure of the solution curves in future lessons. But for now, it's helpful to realize that for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n0 < y(t) < a.\n$$\n\n \n\nAn example of a logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = 10y(50-y).\n$$\n\nHere, $k = 10$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f5791475-4f91-5859-9d5b-1f20283522ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 982 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a86bd27c-35f7-56a0-9dd4-1415fb4b1ae3" :step/math-academy-id 1925 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101786}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101785 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100942 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100777 :question/id #uuid "a16c2c4f-38c3-5e8c-8962-3ad6c956748a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a46e688b-117f-5394-bcbb-fbfb7a5b6c7b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447301}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Logistic Growth Equation"} :step/id #uuid "fa88498c-b724-5dfa-b5a3-3f4d0dbea254" :step/math-academy-id 27104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101786 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100779 :question/id #uuid "29ee4686-7e38-5000-9d89-58e572f84cd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7404"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a4751337-93bf-5b47-8b3f-32100820b19a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051017}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447298} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523982}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Logistic Growth Differential Equation"} :step/id #uuid "257b95d0-a448-51aa-936a-9acc386bda17" :step/math-academy-id 27468 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101800 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101996 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to create a model of the deer population in a forest. Let's denote the number of deer in the forest at time $t$ as $P(t).$\n\nImagine that we have two factors that influence the size of the deer population:\n\n- Unconstrained, the rate at which the deer population increases is proportional to the size of the deer population (i.e., exponential growth).\n\n- However, as the deer population increases, food resources become more scarce. The forest cannot sustain an arbitrarily large number of deer. Therefore, we must specify the maximum number of deer that the forest can sustain. This maximum number is the forest's carrying capacity, denoted $a,$ as before.\n\nTherefore, we say that the rate of change of the deer population $P(t)$ is **jointly proportional** to *both* the deer population $P$ itself *and* the remaining room for growth, which is $a-P.$ This gives us the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = kP(a-P).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "136097ca-5c5c-587e-918d-decbcb156cbf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5720 :tutorial/title "Modeling Situations Using a Logistic Growth Equation"} :step/id #uuid "80e2d4b5-6d7a-5649-8978-26fea11f0ae7" :step/math-academy-id 35117 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101785}}] :activity/title "Logistic Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101030}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524072} :activity/id #uuid "85f9efd2-54dd-542f-9fb0-3457207d9896" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048578 :topic/id #uuid "aae56e33-e57d-5843-b31c-fcba98a1ca50" :topic/math-academy-id 1163} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524056 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253560 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a356ff5-da1f-5dea-9c22-ac42a978f1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250914} {:answer/id #uuid "3882ae11-e665-5f98-b13e-856ea3ff067a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251023} {:answer/id #uuid "65ba5d91-2960-5f9c-b94f-a9c1cfb957b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251388} {:answer/id #uuid "81afc71e-2e81-5e1d-9747-fb7bccbefe35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251647} {:answer/id #uuid "f220ea73-cdb5-5817-8387-4873699ce350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251023} :answer-field/id #uuid "225256aa-a878-5dbd-ac6b-c6508ad4090e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6e07b3cd-6441-55ba-89c7-eb3d668f886b" :question/math-academy-id "q-119289" :question/problem "A pest infestation is spreading through a land with $300$ trees. The rate of growth in the number of trees $P(t)$ infected with the pest is jointly proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ measured in months,  and the number of uninfected trees. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected trees is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected trees, the number of infected trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the number of trees that are infected is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4245bf2c-f044-5000-a8dc-9e8cf792d0e0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bfabfe5-1bde-5d94-bc4f-bdd3166c3f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=20-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523379} {:answer/id #uuid "46cc8677-e9f5-50f1-957a-d6c09372ed81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523401} {:answer/id #uuid "55c32120-58ea-521e-945f-a4d006827dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523430} {:answer/id #uuid "674fb894-2a3f-511b-92a2-711f65663bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(20-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523466} {:answer/id #uuid "80937afc-7ea6-5bca-890e-a680c897e7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523430} :answer-field/id #uuid "c929a608-d503-51e7-8e16-80118c54e14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83846" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=20.$"} :step/id #uuid "576ab541-b8f7-5797-923d-f3afc45640a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524073}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524072 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101549 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d85e959-a5d2-57fb-865e-ce7038ba9c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(500-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523283} {:answer/id #uuid "3d57d462-ef3d-519d-9fd5-bf23c60439b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523383} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3d47cb-be7b-539e-8964-2c16730aedcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=500-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523509} {:answer/id #uuid "cf651d75-4fcd-5021-9ec0-003f1d0c2149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523689} {:answer/id #uuid "e6223966-2351-5f3e-86f0-c546dcff0000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523738}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523689} :answer-field/id #uuid "09aa265b-d358-5ee1-8520-4f4d7c406505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d36aaa4-be3b-5e83-9941-c6527d829fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83754" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$"} :step/id #uuid "845e97b0-decc-50f2-997f-93c6453f8cf7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524073 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c7ca84a-cf9f-50f8-b341-b5cad100965c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.6P(700\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523411} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee52ea6-c780-5145-af3b-00bf2afb2de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=700\\,000P(0.6-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523481} {:answer/id #uuid "b251d084-9823-5f88-b5bc-44200f475b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=700\\,000P(0.6+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523630} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1b6f5-2157-5ffa-857f-384cd5652759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523684} {:answer/id #uuid "cedd52a2-345c-58b2-928e-6c0218a680ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000+P)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523684} :answer-field/id #uuid "e33bf6b7-b2a2-5c1d-93df-e2640b2be937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186100467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f74431a-7f46-5af6-affc-22cbe39ff9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-83865" :question/problem "An influenza epidemic is spreading through a community of $700\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with the flu is jointly proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ measured in days,  and the number of uninfected people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.6,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected people, the number of infected people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.6,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=700\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have the flu is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "876b041e-48ee-59ed-9570-27aae1e87501" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524097}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524097 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bba37af-8df4-5b8c-a930-80d2186439c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251435} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe50a92-b71b-5769-a939-63ed274c1a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252210} {:answer/id #uuid "dead85fa-fe2f-53e8-b67b-e9c7315851fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252516} {:answer/id #uuid "fd955712-55d7-5520-b9cd-82c788b4c2f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=50-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252778} {:answer/id #uuid "fe08bd02-4725-5feb-946c-394fc94ff25a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=4(50-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251435} :answer-field/id #uuid "569731a1-44d9-5454-8f2b-b1754aaefd2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19423d48-c141-516d-a17c-c34914d7472c" :question/math-academy-id "q-119264" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=4y(50-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50.$"} :step/id #uuid "c8f38971-defd-50cd-9dce-38d71497ce0f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524056}}] :activity/title "Logistic Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523243}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060} :activity/id #uuid "2179f3df-5754-599f-a160-da910b72c2ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712 :topic/id #uuid "b784cbd9-6f0d-5689-9a0e-6e19dfca34c7" :topic/math-academy-id 931} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093431 :tutorial/content "Consider once again the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square that is spanned by these vectors.\n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/8f/8fca8cf6dd5b232d3aed6eeb98fb6c244872c55425673cf3ac1441a22e2e29f3.svg) \n\nAs before, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/3f/3f8b5f0f827d359f32a4311021dc48947447133918d48a2f1cad511531b13c81.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/41/4195c8cd2f22fb0f84056226fb7b28c5f9944f747ca3230bf187752829b2a04e.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "70a75b2e-3fa9-5aa9-b308-79b56d57fae7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3542 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "f91bce30-93ef-5342-8485-0619a059a2b3" :step/math-academy-id 20041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092065 :question/id #uuid "a74be952-4dd4-5a7f-a8a4-de68977c5bb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5457"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420105}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "7452acd6-f07a-5cbb-a6a1-887429d397a8" :step/math-academy-id 20045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093432 :tutorial/content "Consider the standard basis ![](images/80/80dd67cd707fb8f6430942bd565fd4da3d7690b9013aa0813475caa3afcfe12b.svg) of the Euclidean plane ![](images/8b/8b0d045a28e5e130e31d4c31b5b7a36f3c46dc9fccfa55c2a73cba7e04a5951c.svg) and the unit square spanned by these two vectors. \n\nLet's define a linear transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by its standard matrix ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) given by \n\n![](images/c6/c6865c5373231451885b3d76e3c8151e9c707462a8b405d9b014b1bda17065a8.svg)\n\nWe can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) by computing the matrix products, as follows:\n![](images/5b/5b8e1c8e03ebb60716eaa2fefded5e7fc286f6de9cffdece8200f6f56c2bbe8e.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c2dc9f76bb23225cec0a4f62541d8a74c4c4b16d2e2f7c37e8d36922d97c59f0.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) about the origin.**\n\nNote that we typically refer to rotations in the counterclockwise sense. However, in this case, the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a3/a3bf307d258c37da47916be4e50553ef612072912cd6ed8dc24fddafc6c3722a.svg) (or ![](images/86/8662109ab709179527b5d1768c0f42a8fb4d6493e41082c5317be360ec4169dd.svg) radians) *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "410fc9b7-ab2d-52ba-9b26-7afd34fdf9e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3545 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "68d9dc46-2592-5260-a1bb-53e6b885e53f" :step/math-academy-id 20046 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092066 :question/id #uuid "e945271e-2180-559b-9998-53ab9982043c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5458"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050589}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354275}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "9915017c-1452-5136-bb94-65860a727a47" :step/math-academy-id 20047 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093058}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093434 :tutorial/content "Finally, let \n\n![](images/1b/1b840cbdb5b3953dfef9910d34248ea280bf38299e4f0a3d0a78143e7301f159.svg)\n\nbe a matrix of a linear transformation ![](images/2d/2d90be63023ff97e21e1a97ccee6c387437ac2a99787278c6f71f37e3ac86850.svg) As usual, we can find the images of ![](images/a7/a7655c84c060c79a0c0850d5d0537e4b4d7b7826d0339e261f87aa7ed4f09229.svg) under ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) as follows:\n![](images/76/76e24611a2cd8a3d5d01fc0a04a9b7a3d0957db84dd1a45bc2f1f82492fb2efd.svg)\n\nPlotting the vectors ![](images/1f/1f249f835230ce9561402247b4c94e15cae46adc5e7dc4263316da3bfcf2348a.svg) and ![](images/bc/bcfcd6164292fee1aeff0dc8325607fc44ff8fe3fa41d4d3abfb95c17d98db92.svg) and their images ![](images/e5/e5f0a5e6b245b18ea6c8d6f65c82702cba528e740aacd5e97535e5705cea9415.svg) and ![](images/98/982d513f1246c98c5055421fad26619b5fe53085da40a017673554f8c992631d.svg) we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/0f/0fc5f33d0bd7047ab02bd9bc3ea3b00c083ab214f3bc8a51e9cbb6e54dc6b51b.png)\n\nWe see that the transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) represented by the matrix ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) defines a **counterclockwise rotation of ![](images/9d/9daf99b64dcaee73aea5338e639cab6c5c564b5975c3fe5eb525f082e4d660f5.svg) or ![](images/e0/e067125c7fb9ca2d725c3513586ec3ba8572b95b5dfac530bd8d35d116ac2ab0.svg) radians, about the origin.**\n\nThe transformation ![](images/5a/5ab4d8d28ee7c7e99d35b1f9932f168e9311ecc0ca1dc0e494fdf73fa1baf484.svg) can also be considered a rotation of ![](images/a0/a0497a2da4d79804cefd03dfabdca0316f86b4c119506a5afdc57c5e250b3adc.svg) or ![](images/87/873f07caba734ac094497c48f87df79ebf63194809a956ecd0e747bb8b593c39.svg) radians *clockwise* about the origin." :tutorial/id #uuid "49541094-17cb-52e7-bb58-8ee2199ad6d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3547 :tutorial/title "Rotations of 270 Degrees"} :step/id #uuid "d48d44d1-fe67-587c-a56e-251eee6b68f8" :step/math-academy-id 20053 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092114 :question/id #uuid "7091b406-96d1-5663-9575-66f26c6a6267" :question/math-academy-id "e-7186"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048203}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278322} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278324} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423032}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "496d3d86-9264-56e9-a8a9-63d5cf7428bf" :step/math-academy-id 26469 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093059}}] :activity/title "Right-Angle Rotations as Linear Transformations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092430}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300} :activity/id #uuid "bf654963-a5d1-5562-85bc-b7c2fbbe6d40" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130300 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130449 :tutorial/content "Recall that when we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi y \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow suppose we have a parametrically defined curve: \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in \\left[t_1,t_2\\right].\n$$\n\nIn the case of a parametric curve, the arc length element $\\textrm{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following formula for the surface area generated by rotating a parametrically defined curve about the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "36a57433-1c78-58b8-860c-0ea6e3471761" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6113 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c5c27e9e-03cb-5a93-8a56-bd33588960fe" :step/math-academy-id 618749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130301 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7707 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "031d5fb3-1834-595b-8a36-577430a0f9a7" :step/math-academy-id 618750 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130302 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129073 :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050860}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435125} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527172}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "a6a3e1f3-c536-58d3-a81e-7966262884ce" :step/math-academy-id 618751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130303}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130303 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130450 :tutorial/content "When we rotate a curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, the area generated by this surface of revolution is given by\n\n$$\nS =  \\int 2\\pi x \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in the case of a parametrically defined curve \n\n$$\nx = x(t), \\qquad\ny = y(t), \\qquad\nt \\in [t_1,t_2],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x(t) \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "548401c3-a52c-5444-b834-57c50c4bfa72" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6145 :tutorial/title "Surface Area Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "622390da-a9e6-55af-8449-1ceed5d4cec3" :step/math-academy-id 618754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130304}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130304 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129074 :question/id #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9867"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049491}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "ddd7ced1-9fe0-533d-bd7a-9dfbcff663f8" :step/math-academy-id 618755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130305}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129075 :question/id #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9869"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435127} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "2f4e64a8-3397-5e7e-b805-080c59c56440" :step/math-academy-id 618758}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129340}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530558} :activity/id #uuid "f6065dfd-4199-50b0-9334-da4b9f494b0d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049265 :topic/id #uuid "e80c9bca-b203-5866-835e-d0d29bbfdf22" :topic/math-academy-id 1995} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6878ad2d-c3f6-5472-b932-d7a4364f9bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{4}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529841} {:answer/id #uuid "735f35d2-93c2-528a-acc9-d6a1cf48503f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} {:answer/id #uuid "8f18c374-0ec5-5b5a-8aa2-bf73f6a415be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{2}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4866bd0-791b-5423-befb-168b1ad8cf39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530030} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3c2df3-9d88-53e0-973d-199d14e0ed36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{4}{2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529872} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4a256d7-74ef-5f59-aa17-d233ba137369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5349d9de-8c6d-5416-a022-6878977b6004" :question/math-academy-id "q-139057" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "032c215a-e883-53c2-bdc0-c14d20b9401e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530454 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3256b67-7e99-5826-a95a-b55a197e4d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530184} :answer-field/id #uuid "12fa28de-d131-50b4-b0e1-7dd696b0e54f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530230}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f72f786-eb31-5b03-9051-ae70858e9f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-352035" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt[4]{6}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt[4]{6}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{4})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{3}}{t}^{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}(1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2|t|\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{16}{3}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t⟹{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{16}\\text{d}u.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt[4]{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $9$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[4]{6}}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4}{3}{t}^{4}}\\cdot {t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{3}{16}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}[(9)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(27-1) \\\\  & =13\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08362200-3b6f-52bb-9fff-49cbac1aba34" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15a0040-721e-5583-80c4-6b4b6d161036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi(2-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530062} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b596d95-5abf-5a25-8619-bd46ab687c7e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "95891d65-20e2-5732-9bd4-e52bbf49b5d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-352088" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to ${t}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(t/2)}\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{csc}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-2+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{csc}^{2}⁡t-1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[u]}_{\\sqrt{3}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi (2-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a2cc474-bbd9-524c-88cd-2cfdbe4a121b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "276f10df-f807-54a9-b01f-7cf1b357ec66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529665} {:answer/id #uuid "8f170c4b-c5f7-5356-8a5a-6fd851ea13ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529925} {:answer/id #uuid "a53f81c5-8ba3-5b6b-9818-2872dc69946f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} {:answer/id #uuid "b86bdc8d-5bf9-5ea7-8414-b3dab58e55bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530042} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f6ac1e-5d34-5fa1-a3ce-c20f5afa43d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530163}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529990} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8fb9e3-9863-5703-95c1-0d8295fc07d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "497a1af7-923f-5a2e-82d5-147a5d2a1d6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-138945" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3t,\\,y=4t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{{(3)}^{2}+{(4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3t\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =30\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "50c04e39-0863-56d5-a574-6b3bc23fdf01" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530465 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "961eb0d5-8253-50e6-a961-755f2db89486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529944} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3f06b1d-08c6-50bc-8914-e903872c2d7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6528b016-7e7a-5f42-bb80-0fc7063586be" :question/math-academy-id "q-352031" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}t,\\,y=\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,\\sqrt{3}].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}t)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2})=\\frac{1}{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2}t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{1}{2}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{t}^{2}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot {[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}[(4)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(8-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8d943a17-8038-5310-ba12-619cf9e0f21c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530550}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution for Parametric Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529573}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1320.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097774} :activity/id #uuid "acc24286-334e-50b7-b51f-39796fa9c946" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050521 :topic/id #uuid "5036af57-460d-5886-bb25-967e885ae634" :topic/math-academy-id 1140} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097927 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int x\\ln{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\,.\n$$\n\nThe method of integration by parts can help us solve this problem. Let's remind ourselves of the \"by-parts\" formula,\n\n$$\n\\int u v' \\textrm{d}x = uv - \\int v u'\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe usually let $u = x,$ but that would leave us with $v' = \\ln(x)$ which is hard to integrate. So instead, we're going to choose our $u$ and $v'$ slightly differently, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow the formula of integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{u}\\cdot {{x}_{⏟}}_{{v}^{′}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}-\\int \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\int x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we apply integration by parts to integrals containing logarithmic functions, we should *always* identify the logarithmic function with $u,$ not with $v'.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dbf22d18-c638-527f-a085-8fcc19fbfbeb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 897 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e5d3fd98-fbd8-5b6d-a250-876573acf24e" :step/math-academy-id 618388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096475 :question/id #uuid "c7373a16-c90a-5875-8b17-8724542b35ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31a28451-005e-50dc-a7cf-5